      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Base rate/reference class: the strongest benchmark is FY2025 defense-agency contract persistence at about 491.0B. The current USAspending contracts row is 238.6B before the late-fiscal-year contracting closeout, and the earlier same-endpoint snapshot was 111.0B, showing substantial in-year accumulation but not enough history to justify abandoning persistence."
      },
      {
        "kind": "math",
        "text": "Model candidates: persistence candidate point=491.0B using the FY2025 defense-contract benchmark; YTD trajectory candidate is lower because 238.6B through the current snapshot annualizes poorly before the September obligation surge. With no richer resolver-consistent annual history fetched, I select a shrinkage blend rather than a pure linear run rate: point = 0.70*491.0 + 0.30*372.0 = 455.3B, rounded to 455B."
      },
      {
        "kind": "math",
        "text": "Prior/update/interval: prior=FY2025 persistence 491.0B; historical sample=fetched 110.967B earlier FY2026 snapshot, fetched 238.617B current FY2026 snapshot, and 491.0B FY2025 defense-contract benchmark; adjustment components=-36B for FY2026 YTD running below a full persistence path but only partial because defense obligations are back-loaded; interval method=sparse snapshot/reference-class volatility. Successive changes are 238.617-110.967=127.650 and 491.000-238.617=252.383, so sigma = 88.2B from the two-change sample; 1.28*sigma = 112.9B. I narrow modestly to a 95B half-width because the registered target is an aggregate DoD contract series with a strong prior, giving implied bounds 455-95=360B and 455+95=550B."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Counter-consideration: downside risk would land below the interval if continuing-resolution effects, procurement delays, or reporting lag leave a large share of late-FY actions absent from the registered October snapshot. Upside risk would land above the interval if large aircraft, shipbuilding, munitions, or supplemental-appropriation contract actions post before the snapshot and make FY2026 closer to a high-spend closeout year."
      },
